WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.220
Sometimes Margot Robbie slaps people to get&nbsp;
her way, other times she just writes a nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.880
letter. This is the story of her journey&nbsp;
from Down Under to the top of the world.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.680
Margot Robbie was born on July 2,&nbsp;
1990 on the gold coast of Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.400
the daughter of a sugarcane magnate. Her&nbsp;
father Doug left when she was very young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.060
resulting in a strained relationship&nbsp;
between the two, but she still has him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.080
to thank for her upbringing on a rural farm&nbsp;
and for her education at private schools.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.920
Despite having a rich father, Robbie and&nbsp;
her mom Sarie Kessler struggled to get by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.700
after he left. Robbie picked up odd jobs&nbsp;
to make ends meet, including at the fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.900
food chain Subway. She was initially tasked&nbsp;
with slicing vegetables and cleaning cutlery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.340
but then she was promoted to the sandwich&nbsp;
line. As she recalled to Vanity Fair in 2014,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.060
"I was really good at it! I make a&nbsp;
mean Subway. The trick is to spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.600
everything evenly out and cut it so&nbsp;
well that there is never a bad bite."

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.480
Robbie's other jobs included&nbsp;
catering, warehouse work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.260
and a stint at a pharmacy. Then after&nbsp;
she landed her first big acting gig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.500
on an Australian soap opera, Subway&nbsp;
hired her to star in a commercial.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.180
Fans outside Margot Robbie's home country&nbsp;
of Australia probably think of her mainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.440
as the avatar of American celebs Tonya&nbsp;
Harding or Sharon Tate. But back home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.280
she's perhaps even more beloved for her work&nbsp;
on the long-running soap opera Neighbors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.940
on which she played Donna Freedman. The&nbsp;
character became a fan favorite thanks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.140
part to positive bisexual representation&nbsp;
and Robbie's obvious star power.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.700
Originally cast as a one-off role, Donna&nbsp;
soon became a recurring character. Robbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.820
ended up staying on the show for&nbsp;
three years, but then in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.420
she left Australia to pursue a career&nbsp;
in Hollywood. As she told TV Week,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.080
"I want to go to America; it's always&nbsp;
been my goal to work in Hollywood. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.095
the one stage in my life where I have&nbsp;
absolutely nothing holding me down."

00:01:37.095 --> 00:01:42.286
"Years living on Ramsay Street were honestly&nbsp;
some of the best of my entire life."

00:01:42.286 --> 00:01:46.800
After Margott Robbie jetted off to Hollywood, she&nbsp;
initially landed a few small movie roles. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.840
everything changed when director Martin Scorsese&nbsp;
cast her in the 2014 crime epic The Wolf of Wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.400
Street. She starred opposite Leonardo DiCaprio,&nbsp;
whose deranged energy she matched beat for beat.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.360
Robbie reportedly got the part by slapping&nbsp;
DiCaprio in the face during her audition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.680
a move that was actually not in the original&nbsp;
script. As she recalled to Harper's Bazaar,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.120
"In my head I was like, 'You have literally 30&nbsp;
seconds left in this room and if you don't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.560
something impressive nothing will ever come of it.&nbsp;
It's a once-in-a-lifetime chance, just take it.'"

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.380
And thus, that one slap set in motion a storied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.980
career that has seen Robbie land&nbsp;
iconic role after iconic role.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.100
When Robbie signed up for 2016's Suicide Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.820
she didn't know much about the character of Harley&nbsp;
Quinn. As she admitted to the Washington Post,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.880
"I wasn't familiar with the comics.&nbsp;
I had vaguely heard of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.520
but I had no idea that there was&nbsp;
such a big fanbase for Harley."

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.380
Nevertheless, her take on Harley was a hit, and&nbsp;
the character was even reportedly the most Googled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:42.540
Halloween costume of 2016. It was a significant&nbsp;
departure from earlier versions of Harley, as her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:47.580
outfit consisted of fishnets, a studded belt, and&nbsp;
a ripped tee shirt reading "Daddy's Lil Monster."

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.480
Though she initially had no plans to&nbsp;
play Harley again, Robbie leapt at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.740
opportunity to redeem the character with a&nbsp;
feminist spin in 2020's Birds of Prey. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.520
was also an executive producer this time&nbsp;
around. As she noted to Rotten Tomatoes:

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.980
"I thought the industry needed&nbsp;
a girl-gang action film."

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.760
Plenty of fans fell in love with Robbie's&nbsp;
version of Harley Quinn in Suicide Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.040
but if it weren't for the&nbsp;
actress's intense dedication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.240
that could've been the last time any of us&nbsp;
saw the character on screen. So when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.140
time to bring her back for Birds of Prey, she&nbsp;
put in the work to really make it memorable.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.480
The movie was less of a sequel to Suicide Squad&nbsp;
and more of a companion piece exploring Harley's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.380
own offbeat life, and it forced Robbie to&nbsp;
work in multiple roles at once. As one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.280
the founders of LuckyChap Entertainment, she&nbsp;
also served as one of the movie's producers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.760
She ultimately went above and beyond,&nbsp;
getting all the pieces together to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.140
the film a reality and committing to the role&nbsp;
so much that she did most of her own stunts.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.620
Robbie wanted to do as many of her own&nbsp;
action sequences herself as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:48.420
and she brought that same energy when she played&nbsp;
Harley once more in 2021's The Suicide Squad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.680
Her biggest scene in that outing involves&nbsp;
gymnastic-level flexibility as she uses her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.300
feet to pick a lock before escaping from a prison&nbsp;
cell, and she did almost all the work on her own.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.360
"She is like a human Swiss Army knife."

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.480
After Margot Robbie made the&nbsp;
jump from TV star to movie star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.920
she didn't stop there. In between Wolf of Wall&nbsp;
Street and Suicide Squad, she was laying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.820
groundwork for the next phase of her career.&nbsp;
As she explained to Harper's Bazaar in 2018,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.360
"I didn't want to pick up another&nbsp;
script where I was the wife or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.020
girlfriend — just a catalyst for the&nbsp;
male story line. It was uninspiring."

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.380
Her part in Wolf of Wall Street may&nbsp;
have skyrocketed Robbie to fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.100
but it wasn't exactly the kind of role she&nbsp;
was keen on playing over and over again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.140
So she decided to solve that problem of potential&nbsp;
typecasting by starting her own production studio.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.460
LuckyChap Entertainment began operating&nbsp;
in 2014, and it's grown significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.960
since then. Not every one of its films have&nbsp;
been smashing successes, but some, like I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.820
Tonya and Promising Young Woman, have won over&nbsp;
critics, general audiences, and awards groups.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.540
A few years after Wolf of Wall Street,&nbsp;
Robbie starred in another film that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.820
just as important, if not more so, in the overall&nbsp;
transformation of her career. Released in 2017, I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.780
Tonya marked a number of firsts for her. It was&nbsp;
the first time that she played a leading role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.380
and it was also the first time that her&nbsp;
production company, LuckyChap Entertainment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.520
helped bring a project to life. In many ways,&nbsp;
it was a make-or-break moment for the company.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.960
Written by Steven Rogers and directed by&nbsp;
Craig Gillespie, the biopic follows the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.280
of figure skater Tonya Harding, so Robbie had&nbsp;
to put in some extra work for this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.480
movie by learning how to skate. Ultimately,&nbsp;
the actress and her team struck gold with I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.040
Tonya. It was a modest hit at the box office and a&nbsp;
major player at the Oscars, with Robbie nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.240
for Lead Actress, and her co-star Allison&nbsp;
Janney winning for Best Supporting Actress.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:35.160
"I was loved… for a minute."

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.640
Based on true stories, 2019's Bombshell explores&nbsp;
a culture of sexual harassment that proliferated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.080
at Fox News under CEO Roger Ailes. Several&nbsp;
of the roles are based on actual people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.700
but Margot Robbie's part of Kayla was a composite&nbsp;
character with multiple real-life inspirations.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.740
Robbie has admitted that Bombshell was something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.220
of a wake-up call for her. As she&nbsp;
told Variety in November 2022,

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.940
"I realized that I — as a person with&nbsp;
an established position in the industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.760
financially set up and self-sufficient — I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.760
know the definition of sexual&nbsp;
harassment, and that's shocking."

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.020
Robbie also told Entertainment Weekly that she&nbsp;
had a tough time getting inside Kayla's head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.740
and when she was on set, the work&nbsp;
didn't get any easier. As she put it,

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.440
"The emotional side was difficult&nbsp;
in a different kind of way."

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.260
All of that hard work ultimately paid off, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.840
with Bombshell earning Robbie&nbsp;
her second Oscar nomination.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.120
Margot Robbie is a massive fan of director&nbsp;
Quentin Tarantino. Way back before she broke big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.540
she made a plan to write him a letter when&nbsp;
she felt that she was ready to star in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:34.680
of his films. That moment came in 2017,&nbsp;
after she saw her own performance in I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.160
Tonya. So she finally wrote that letter&nbsp;
that she'd been thinking about for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.180
and it just so happened to land in front of&nbsp;
Tarantino as he was beginning to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.660
casting for his next movie, 2019's&nbsp;
Once Upon a Time... in Hollywood.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.860
Robbie didn't know it at the time, but several&nbsp;
of Tarantino's friends had already floated her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.520
name for the role of Sharon Tate. And so, he&nbsp;
reached out and invited Robbie to his home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.300
where she spent hours reading through the script&nbsp;
he'd just finished. By the time she was done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.720
she was ready to play the role, and he&nbsp;
was more than willing to offer it to her.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.720
"I'm in the movie. I'm Sharon Tate."

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.820
As an actor, producer, and business owner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.600
Margot Robbie doesn't exactly have much&nbsp;
time to herself, but she does have a plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.740
for what to do when work finally comes to&nbsp;
an end. As she admitted to Vogue in 2023,

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.480
"All I want to do is live on a train."

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.220
Robbie derives quite a bit of joy in sitting back&nbsp;
and staring out the window while her train rolls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:24.900
through the countryside. In 2022, she and her&nbsp;
husband Tom Ackerly took a ride on the Orient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.680
Express, which had been a longtime goal of&nbsp;
Robbie's. She even spent the trip watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.420
the 1974 movie version of Murder on the Orient&nbsp;
Express and seeing how her travels compared.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.040
As it turns out, an obsession with trains&nbsp;
might be a bit of a family trait. Back in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.340
Robbie took a trip back to her native&nbsp;
Australia to spend some time with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.880
sister. Rather than relaxing by a pool,&nbsp;
they hopped on board the country's famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.060
Ghan train, which makes a four-day&nbsp;
trip through the Australian outback.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.020
Margot Robbie has already gotten to work&nbsp;
with some of her biggest Hollywood heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.500
like Martin Scorsese and Quentin&nbsp;
Tarantino. But she hasn't quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.540
crossed every dream partnership off her wish list.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.700
In a 2022 Vanity Fair profile, she mentioned some&nbsp;
notable directors she'd still like to work with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.680
including Paul Thomas Anderson, Bong Joon Ho,&nbsp;
and Céline Sciamma. At this point in her career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.980
it's hard to imagine that Robbie would have&nbsp;
much difficulty getting to work with anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:15.180
else she wants. She's one of the biggest stars&nbsp;
in Hollywood, and as a producer at LuckyChap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.620
Entertainment, she has plenty of say about what&nbsp;
projects she's going to work on next, after all.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.380
Despite the enviable position&nbsp;
she finds herself in today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.620
Robbie still feels just as excited&nbsp;
about getting to work as she did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:30.420
during her early days on Australian TV.&nbsp;
As she admitted to Net-a-Porter in 2018,

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.900
"I've been on sets for pretty much the last&nbsp;
10 years and I still walk on and think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:37.800
'This is soooooooo coooool! Look at&nbsp;
that! That's amazing! Oh my gosh!'"

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.980
Margot Robbie might seem like the&nbsp;
perfect actress to play Barbie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:45.360
but that decision wasn't always obvious to&nbsp;
her. As she admitted to Vogue in May 2023,

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.600
"This is going to sound stupid, but I&nbsp;
really didn't even think about playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.640
Barbie until years into developing the project."

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.300
Robbie's work on the Barbie movie&nbsp;
actually began from a producer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.720
perspective. She didn't really have much&nbsp;
of a personal history with the Mattel toys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:00.540
so it wasn't exactly something she'd&nbsp;
been dreaming of her whole life.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:05.220
"I think we want, more than anything, everyone to&nbsp;
kind of have the experience they want to have."

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.060
Robbie helped get director Greta Gerwig involved&nbsp;
in the project, and the two of them started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.840
brainstorming ideas for who fit their idea of&nbsp;
the character. The first person on that list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.520
was actually Gal Gadot, as they believed that she&nbsp;
embodied "Barbie energy." Ultimately, there ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.960
up being multiple versions of Barbies in the film,&nbsp;
although Gadot isn't among them. And with Robbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:26.160
taking the lead, she fits into the role as well&nbsp;
as Barbie fits into her Dream House. While it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.060
wasn't the plan from the beginning, it definitely&nbsp;
seems like it was always fated to go this way.

